<p>The U.S. Army said Sunday that searchers had recovered the remains of U.S. Army 1st Lt. Kendrick Lamont Key Jr., one of two American soldiers reported missing after taking part in the African Lion 2026 military exercise in southern Morocco. Moroccan searchers found the remains Saturday in the Atlantic Ocean within about 1 mile, or 1.6 km, of where Key disappeared on May 2 near a cliff in the Cap Draa training area. Reports citing the Army said Key, 27, was from Richmond, Virginia, and served as a platoon leader in an artillery unit. Moroccan military personnel flew his remains to the morgue at Moulay El Hassan Military Hospital in Guelmim as arrangements proceeded for repatriation to the United States. A second U.S. soldier remained missing, and U.S., Moroccan and allied teams continued search operations.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/1P-ePoRA5YJxynIPj77eqGWGHV6_Uo6JpmNS0t8qB-Cb9qOTD3Vonolx4Z2pXrKo-3BteJ6FGRRl7MziWL_OaCaQUP4PmT4NgErZL8-65XEGdoVJk6QG4b3yLQgboYvgfHMX7Q6g5W0iK6HPD5_-x6wuwo8b-LFejQw1gdv7YzMjz5ixXvip5Vq5ocTHPU-WCk8iFu94WbbaC4i7rr07hBtX0znFqZIYyohSAInqcoruewNYO8GLLOjWJgCYvCtEBYc' alt='U.S. Army 1st Lt. Kendrick Lamont Key Jr.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Search scale: U.S. Africa Command said the search covered more than 8,000 square kilometers of sea and coastal terrain and used more than 10 aircraft and an underwater vehicle.</li><li>Exercise size: African Lion 2026 involved nearly 5,000 service members from more than 40 countries, according to U.S. Africa Command figures cited in reports.</li><li>Preliminary assessment: An unnamed U.S. military official told AFP the two soldiers may have fallen into the ocean, the incident was viewed as an accident, and terrorism was ruled out.</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/body-of-missing-us-soldier-recovered-in-morocco'>Body of missing US soldier recovered in Morocco</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/